speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I wish to take only a few minutes of the time of the house in speaking on this resolution. It is certainly the right of a free people to voluntarily surrender their rights in times of national emergency. It is certainly also the right of a free people to have this voluntary surrender supervised by their elected representatives to the end that these rights will be given up only when this is really required.
The bill of rights, in part II, section 6, sets out that the operative sections of the War Measures Act, when brought into effect by the governor in council, shall be laid before parliament and parliament itself shall agree that the government shall have these all-inclusive and overriding powers in connection with the lives, property and movements of citizens of the country.
When one reads the War Measures Act one discovers it is rather a frightening act in the degree to which free people voluntarily give into the hands of the government the right

to do whatever that government may wish to do. Section 3 (1) of the War Measures Act provides:
-it is hereby declared that the powers of the governor in council shall extend to all matters coming within the classes of subjects hereinafter enumerated, that is to say:
(a)	censorship and the control and suppression of publications, writings, maps, plans, photographs, communications and means of communication;
(b)	arrest, detention, exclusion and deportation;
(c)	control of the harbours, ports and territorial waters of Canada and the movements of vessels;
(d)	transportation by land, air, or water and the control of the transport of persons and things;
(e)	trading, exportation, importation, production and manufacture;
(f)	appropriation, control, forfeiture and disposition of property and of the use thereof;
Then it says:
All orders and regulations made under this section shall have the force of law,-
This means that the government, by executive decree, can imprison or report people and seize property. In short, the government has all the powers of a dictator state. These powers are given by a free people; they are given voluntarily; they are surrendered in the national interest during a time of emergency.
Hon. members will notice that the wording of this act contains the phrase "real or apprehended". The bill of rights puts a limitation on this. The limitation is that these powers shall be confirmed by parliament and that parliament shall say whether it is a real emergency such as requires that these overriding powers be given to the executive whereby this free nation voluntarily surrenders to the government, of its own free will, all of the rights which have been won over hundreds of years in seeking a truly democratic status.
It does seem to me, Mr. Speaker, that the work of this committee will be most important in trying to marry two concepts and in trying to reconcile two points of view; one as expressed in the War Measures Act, that in times of emergency the state must come first; and the other as expressed in the bill of rights, that the rights of an individual must be protected against the state. These two viewpoints are not incompatible, but it does mean that perhaps the committee will have to spell out in more detail how they shall be reconciled.
I believe that in bringing forward this resolution the Prime Minister has married together the magnificent concept of the bill of rights and the very practical and utilitarian measures carried in our statutes by the War Measures Act.

